{"qid":"Q858841","names":["Kingdom of Croatia"],"title":"Kingdom of Croatia","endonym":null,"capsule":"The medieval Kingdom of Croatia emerged as an independent realm under Duke Branimir (879–892) and reached its apex under King Tomislav, who united Pannonian and Dalmatian Croatia and was recognized as rex Croatorum around 925. At its height the kingdom controlled the eastern Adriatic hinterland and much of modern-day Croatia, and it produced a distinct Glagolitic ecclesiastical tradition. After King Dmitar Zvonimir died in 1089 without a clear successor, a succession crisis opened the door to Hungarian intervention; by the Pacta conventa of 1102, King Coloman of Hungary was crowned Croatian king, joining the two realms in a personal union that ended Croatian sovereignty.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The kingdom's territorial extent is moderately attested through chronicle sources and papal correspondence, but precise boundaries fluctuated and scholarly reconstructions vary, particularly for the Pannonian northern fringe.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Croatia (925–1102)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Croatia_(925%E2%80%931102)"},{"label":"Britannica: Croatia — History","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Croatia/History"},{"label":"Neven Budak, 'Croatia in the Early Middle Ages', in East Central and Eastern Europe in the Early Middle Ages (University of Michigan Press, 2005)","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/10.3998/mpub.17596"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Pacta conventa (Croatia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacta_conventa_(Croatia)"}],"britann